ne preferred B5. This was the first big drop, and the game never recovered from it."}, {"turn": 17, "text": "H5 lost 8.7 points and took the lead from -19.2 to -27.9. Opus 5.5 was already well behind and kept extending on the right, where the engine kept preferring B5 on the left."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "F9 lost 23.7 points, dropping the lead from -19.5 to -43.3. Opus 5.5 had a wall along row 8 and the engine wanted F2 instead. The 14k's G9 answer lost 22.6 points of its own and gave most of it back (-43.3 to -20.7)."}, {"turn": 45, "text": "C2 was the worst move of the game, losing 32.1 points and sending the lead from -21.3 to -53.4. Opus 5.5 had been feeding stones into the corner along the A and B files, and the engine's choice here was F2."}, {"turn": 46, "text": "The 14k answered with C1, which lost 31.8 points and brought the lead back from -53.4 to -21.5. The two near-equal blunders cancelled out. That left Opus 5.5 still about 21 points behind."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py places one move the player names, removes captured stones, and prints each group's liberties, and libs.py only prints group liberties for the pasted board. The player looped over a few hand-picked candidates in the shell, but each run was a single-move liberty check with no scoring, ranking, or look-ahead beyond that move.", "stop": false}}